        <div class="weekly-secnum">Opening finding</div>
        <h2>J.P. Morgan Payments: "A prompt expresses intent; it does not enforce authority."</h2>
        <p class="weekly-lead-p">On 29 September J.P. Morgan published a piece by Zack Anderson, Chief Data and Analytics Officer for Payments and Global Banking.<sup><a href="#s1">1</a></sup> Instructions constrain an agent, it says, "only if the tools, communications channels, and execution paths enforce them."</p>
        <p>In the design it describes, an execution service "must enforce the decision before cash moves, through a path the agent cannot bypass or reconfigure."</p>
        <p>Its test: a placement with an unapproved counterparty must fail even when it preserves the liquidity buffer, and fail again through a second agent or a stored message.</p>

        <div class="weekly-figure">
          <img
            src="/weekly/jpmorgan-prompt-is-not-a-control.jpg"
            alt="J.P. Morgan Payments newsroom page dated September 29, 2026, headlined A prompt is not a control, with the opening paragraph stating that a prompt expresses intent and does not enforce authority."
