    <section class="weekly-section">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="weekly-secnum">04 / Enforcement Evidence and DAL Impact</div>
        <h2>Prototypes bind a human decision to execution</h2>
        <p>ContractWarden, posted 29 September, is a Linux reference monitor. A model proposes an asset contract, "but a human makes the final choice," and an execution gate "binds the contract to a concrete task before untrusted code runs." All 570 test runs passed.<sup><a href="#s8">8</a></sup> Research prototype.</p>
        <p>ActionGuard, posted 30 September, denies each tool call before execution unless a separate reviewer finds the user request justifies it. Attack success fell 70.44%.<sup><a href="#s9">9</a></sup> The reviewer is a model, not a recorded approval.</p>

        <h3>Conditional assessment</h3>
        <p><strong>Services Australia.</strong> Holding "run commands" and "write files" until authority for that exact target was verified could have stopped the writes, if every outbound path, including the non-public route the model found, passed through the check and the receiving system rejected calls lacking authority.</p>
        <p><strong>AgentScope.</strong> A runtime that refuses execute_python_code without a valid token for that exact code would stop the remote execution. A check inside the agent loop alone fails; the vulnerable path skips it.</p>

        <p class="weekly-status">DAL-X status: the current demo runs end to end in a simulated enterprise environment. Production enforcement controls are still being completed.<sup><a href="#s10">10</a></sup></p>
      </div>
    </section>

